ctural_shift\",\n403\t        \"query\": \"double/debiased machine learning cross-fitting target parameter\",\n404\t        \"rationale\": \"Cross-fitted nuisance estimation with debiased inference motivates the recalibration step and its validity argument.\"\n405\t      },\n406\t      {\n407\t        \"category\": \"core_method\",\n408\t        \"query\": \"isotonic regression calibration of predictions\",\n409\t        \"rationale\": \"Isotonic and binned calibrators are the main recalibration maps g(Yhat) in the best-scoring method.\"\n410\t      },\n411\t      {\n412\t        \"category\": \"architectural_shift\",\n413\t        \"query\": \"stacking nonnegative least squares combining regression estimators\",\n414\t        \"rationale\": \"Cycle 3 uses NNLS-stacked calibrator libraries; need the stacking reference.\"\n415\t      },\n416\t      {\n417\t        \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\",\n418\t        \"query\": \"control variates variance reduction Monte Carlo regression adjustment survey sampling\",\n419\t        \"rationale\": \"PPI++ and recalibration are control-variate / model-assisted estimators; connects width gains to classical difference and regression estimators.\"\n420\t      },\n421\t      {\n422\t        \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n423\t        \"query\": \"Galaxy Zoo 2 morphological classifications; Retiring Adult folktables census datasets\",\n424\t        \"rationale\": \"Dataset provenance for the ppi-python example data used in evaluation; to be split by the retriever into separate dataset lookups.\"\n425\t      }\n426\t    ],\n427\t    \"result_driven_themes\": [\n428\t      \"Width gains over PPI++ are small on real datasets (log width ratio about 0.003-0.03) but larger on synthetic nonlinear miscalibration (about 0.07)\",\n429\t      \"Large gain over the classical interval (log ratio about 0.47-0.49) confirms the predictor is informative\",\n430\t      \"Coverage stays near nominal (pooled about 0.94-0.95) with small-n finite-sample issues, motivating t-reference and variance-estimation corrections\",\n431\t      \"Predictor miscalibration limits how much PPI++ can gain; recalibration helps only when the Yhat-Y relation is nonlinear\"\n432\t    ],\n433\t    \"status\": \"ready\"\n434\t  },\n435\t  \"reference_query_plans\": [\n436\t    {\n437\t      \"architectural_shifts\": [\n438\t        \"From a single global linear control-variate weight (PPI/PPI++) to a nonparametric calibrated control variate g(Yhat) fit by cross-fitting\",\n439\t        \"From one cross-fit to repeated cross-fit with stacked calibrators\",\n440\t        \"From label-based calibration to a label-free basis control variate (did not beat calibration)\"\n441\t      ],\n442\t      \"benchmarks\": [\n443\t        \"ppi-python example data: galaxies (Galaxy Zoo 2 spiral labels)\",\n444\t        \"forest (Amazon deforestation)\",\n445\t        \"alphafold (protein disorder)\",\n446\t        \"gene_expression (promoter expression)\",\n447\t        \"Known-truth synthetic data\"\n448\t      ],\n449\t      \"central_topic\": \"Whether confidence intervals for a population mean can be shortened, at valid nominal coverage, beyond prediction-powered inference when labels, unlabeled data and the pretrained predictor are all fixed, using cross-fitted recalibration of the predictor output as a control variate.\",\n450\t      \"comparators\": [\n451\t        \"Classical labeled-only t/CLT interval\",\n452\t        \"Prediction-Powered Inference (PPI)\",\n453\t        \"PPI++ (tuned lambda, lambda=1 variant)\",\n454\t        \"Cross-prediction-powered inference\",\n455\t        \"Stratified PPI\",\n456\t        \"Double/debiased ML and semiparametric efficient estimation with missing outcomes\"\n457\t      ],\n458\t      \"core_methods\": [\n459\t        \"Cross-fitted recalibrated PPI mean intervals (isotonic / binned / stacked calibrators g(Yhat) fit on labeled data)\",\n460\t        \"Repeated cross-fitting with NNLS-stacked calibrator library and split-dispersion variance\",\n461\t        \"Label-free spline-basis control-variate PPI with PRESS selection (weaker result)\",\n462\t        \"Power-tuned PPI++ shrinkage with PPI++ fallback selection\",\n463\t        \"Efficiency-bound / semiparametric theory for the mean with auxiliary predictions\"\n464\t      ],\n465\t      \"forbidden_query_patterns\": [\n466\t        \"generic optimization or benchmarking phrases\",\n467\t        \"scientific method / paper-writing / automated research phrasing\",\n468\t        \"campaign order, cycle, milestone or search-history phrasing\",\n469\t        \"any query aimed at the withheld published paper on shortening intervals with fixed labels, unlabeled data and predictor, its title, authors, or citing pages\",\n470\t        \"active labeling / extra-label acquisition as a main theme (violates the fixed-label constraint)\",\n471\t        \"deep learning architecture surveys unrelated to the tabular scalar-mean setting\"\n472\t      ],\n473\t      \"queries\": [\n474\t        {\n475\t          \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n476\t          \"query\": \"prediction-powered inference confidence intervals machine learning predictions\",\n477\t          \"rationale\": \"Primary PPI baseline and setting; the paper's main comparator.\"\n478\t        },\n479\t        {\n480\t          \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n481\t          \"query\": \"PPI++ efficient prediction-powered inference tuned power\",\n482\t          \"rationale\": \"PPI++ with data-driven lambda and the lambda=1 variant are the key width baselines in every reported log-ratio.\"\n483\t        },\n484\t        {\n485\t          \"category\": \"core_method\",\n486\t          \"query\": \"cross-prediction-powered inference cross-fitting\",\n487\t          \"rationale\": \"Cross-fitting is the core device of the proposed method; closest prior use of sample splitting with PPI.\"\n488\t        },\n489\t        {\n490\t          \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n491\t          \"query\": \"stratified prediction-powered inference\",\n492\t          \"rationale\": \"Stratified PPI is a comparator that also uses the predictor to reduce variance via binning.\"\n493\t        },\n494\t        {\n495\t          \"category\": \"core_method\",\n496\t          \"query\": \"semiparametric efficient estimation of the mean with missing outcomes and auxiliary predictions\",\n497\t          \"rationale\": \"Efficiency-bound theory for the mean with unlabeled covariate/prediction data underpins the width-ratio analysis and the optimal control variate.\"\n498\t        },\n499\t        {\n500\t          \"category\": \"architectural_shift\",\n501\t          \"query\": \"double/debiased machine learning cross-fitting target parameter\",\n502\t          \"rationale\": \"Cross-fitted nuisance estimation with debiased inference motivates the recalibration step and its validity argument.\"\n503\t        },\n504\t        {\n505\t          \"category\": \"core_method\",\n506\t          \"query\": \"isotonic regression calibration of predictions\",\n507\t          \"rationale\": \"Isotonic and binned calibrators are the main recalibration maps g(Yhat) in the best-scoring method.\"\n508\t        },\n509\t        {\n510\t          \"category\": \"architectural_shift\",\n511\t          \"query\": \"stacking nonnegative least squares combining regression estimators\",\n512\t          \"rationale\": \"Cycle 3 uses NNLS-stacked calibrator libraries; need the stacking reference.\"\n513\t        },\n514\t        {\n515\t          \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\",\n516\t          \"query\": \"control variates variance reduction Monte Carlo regression adjustment survey sampling\",\n517\t          \"rationale\": \"PPI++ and recalibration are control-variate / model-assisted estimators; connects width gains to classical difference and regression estimators.\"\n518\t        },\n519\t        {\n520\t          \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n521\t          \"query\": \"Galaxy Zoo 2 morphological classifications; Retiring Adult folktables census datasets\",\n522\t          \"rationale\": \"Dataset provenance for the ppi-python example data used in evaluation; to be split by the retriever into separate dataset lookups.\"\n523\t        }\n524\t      ],\n525\t      \"result_driven_themes\": [\n526\t        \"Width gains over PPI++ are small on real datasets (log width ratio about 0.003-0.03) but larger on synthetic nonlinear miscalibration (about 0.07)\",\n527\t        \"Large gain over the classical interval (log ratio about 0.47-0.49) confirms the predictor is informative\",\n528\t        \"Coverage stays near nominal (pooled about 0.94-0.95) with small-n finite-sample issues, motivating t-reference and variance-estimation corrections\",\n529\t        \"Predictor miscalibration limits how much PPI++ can gain; recalibration helps only when the Yhat-Y relation is nonlinear\"\n530\t      ],\n531\t      \"status\": \"ready\"\n532\t    },\n533\t    {\n534\t      \"architectural_shifts\": [\n535\t        \"From a single global linear control-variate weight (PPI/PPI++) to a nonparametric calibrated control variate g(Yhat) fit by cross-fitting\",\n536\t        \"From one cross-fit to repeated cross-fit with stacked calibrators\",\n537\t        \"From label-based calibration to a label-free basis control variate (did not beat calibration)\"\n538\t      ],\n539\t      \"benchmarks\": [\n540\t        \"ppi-python example data: galaxies (Galaxy Zoo 2 spiral labels)\",\n541\t        \"forest (Amazon deforestation)\",\n542\t        \"alphafold (protein disorder)\",\n543\t        \"gene_expression (promoter expression)\",\n544\t        \"Known-truth synthetic data\"\n545\t      ],\n546\t      \"central_topic\": \"Whether confidence intervals for a population mean can be shortened, at valid nominal coverage, beyond prediction-powered inference when labels, unlabeled data and the pretrained predictor are all fixed, using cross-fitted recalibration of the predictor output as a control variate.\",\n547\t      \"comparators\": [\n548\t        \"Classical labeled-only t/CLT interval\",\n549\t        \"Prediction-Powered Inference (PPI)\",\n550\t        \"PPI++ (tuned lambda, lambda=1 variant)\",\n551\t        \"Cross-prediction-powered inference\",\n552\t        \"Stratified PPI\",\n553\t        \"Double/debiased M\n554\t\n555\t[truncated after 14000 chars]\n556\t```\n557\t\n558\t## manuscript source excerpt\n559\t\n560\t```tex\n561\t\\documentclass{article}\n562\t\n563\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n564\t\\usepackage{xfp}\n565\t\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n566\t\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n567\t\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{\n568\t  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}\n569\t  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}\n570\t  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}\n571\t  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}\n572\t  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}\n573\t  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}\n574\t  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}\n575\t  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}\n576\t}\n577\t\n578\t \n579\t\\usepackage{paper,times}\n580\t\n581\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n582\t\\usepackage{url}\n583\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n584\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n585\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n586\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n587\t\n588\t\\newcommand{\\Yhat}{\\hat Y}\n589\t\\newcommand{\\E}{\\mathbb{E}}\n590\t\\newcommand{\\Var}{\\mathrm{Var}}\n591\t\\newcommand{\\Cov}{\\mathrm{Cov}}\n592\t\n593\t\\title{How Much Shorter Can a Prediction-Powered Mean Interval Be? A Cross-Fitted Recalibrated Interval Is Slightly Narrower than PPI++ with Fixed Inputs}\n594\t\\author{Anonymous authors}\n595\t\n596\t\\begin{document}\n597\t\\maketitle\n598\t\n599\t\\begin{abstract}\n600\tPrediction-powered inference (PPI) combines a small labeled sample, a large unlabeled sample and a fixed pretrained predictor into a valid confidence interval for a population mean. We ask how much further such an interval can be shortened when labels, unlabeled data and predictor are all held fixed. Writing PPI++ as a control-variate estimator, we show that the attainable gain is governed by the gap between the squared correlation of $Y$ with $\\Yhat$ and the correlation ratio of $Y$ given $\\Yhat$, plus a clipping effect on the tuning weight. We evaluate a cross-fitted recalibration (isotonic or binned) added as a second control variate, with a fallback to PPI++, on a sealed evaluation of four real pools and known-truth synthetic regimes. The interval is 2.2\\% narrower than PPI++ (geometric mean over five families), 6.8\\% narrower than PPI with $\\lambda{=}1$ and 30\\% narrower than the labeled-only interval; per-family reductions range from 0.2\\% to 4.0\\%. Pooled coverage is 0.934 (lowest family 0.91), below the nominal 0.95 although not rejected by the evaluator's exact tests, and the observed narrowing exceeds our population-level ceiling in four of five families. The evidence supports a small, regime-dependent gain; it does not show that the gain is free of a coverage cost.\n601\t\\end{abstract}\n602\t\n603\t\\section{Introduction}\n604\tIn ecological remote sensing, galaxy morphology and census analysis, a scientist often holds three things: a small set of gold-standard labels, a large set of records without labels, and a pretrained predictor that cannot be modified. Prediction-powered inference \\citep{Angelopoulos2023PredictionpoweredI} and its power-tuned form PPI++ \\citep{Angelopoulos2023PPIEP} use the predictor on both samples to produce confidence intervals that remain valid for any predictor and are shorter than the labeled-only interval when the predictor is informative.\n605\t\n606\tWe study a deliberately narrow question. \\emph{With the labeled set, the unlabeled set and the predictor all fixed, can a valid mean interval be shortened below PPI++? By how much, and under which conditions does it help or fail?} Fixing the inputs excludes active labeling \\citep{Zrnic2024ActiveSI}, retraining the predictor, and adding covariates or data; the only remaining freedom is how $(Y,\\Yhat)$ pairs and unlabeled predictions are turned into an interval. The labeled-only data and the predictions are all that any such interval can use.\n607\t\n608\tOur route is a nested one. PPI++ is a control-variate estimator whose control variate is $\\Yhat$ with a scalar weight clipped to $[0,1]$. We add a second control variate $g(\\Yhat)$, a recalibration of the prediction fitted on the labeled data by cross-fitting, and let a variance-minimizing weight vector combine the two. A selection rule falls back to PPI++ unless a recalibrated interval is at least 1\\% shorter than the PPI++ interval. The question then reduces to how much variance the second control variate can remove.\n609\t\n610\t\\paragraph{Contributions.}\n611\t\\begin{itemize}\n612\t\\item \\textbf{Headroom analysis.} For control-variate mean intervals with fixed inputs we give the asymptotic variance in closed form (Section~\\ref{sec:theory}). Relative to PPI++ the gain depends on two quantities only: the clipping of the tuning weight, and the gap $\\eta^2-\\rho^2$ between the correlation ratio of $Y$ on $\\Yhat$ and the squared Pearson correlation. We list the conditions under which the gain is zero; a two-valued predictor removes the nonlinearity term but not necessarily the clipping term.\n613\t\\item \\textbf{A recalibrated PPI interval} with isotonic and binned recalibrators, cross-fitting, Student-$t$ critical values and a 1\\%-margin fallback to PPI++ (Section~\\ref{sec:method}).\n614\t\\item \\textbf{A sealed evaluation} on four real pools and four known-truth synthetic regimes, scored against classical, PPI ($\\lambda{=}1$) and PPI++ intervals, with the width gain, the coverage evidence and the population-level ceiling reported side by side (Section~\\ref{sec:results}).\n615\t\\item \\textbf{A calibrated conclusion.} The recalibrated interval is narrower than PPI++ by 2.2\\% on average, but the average exceeds the population-level ceiling and coverage point estimates sit below 0.95. We state precisely what the evidence does and does not support (Section~\\ref{sec:discussion}).\n616\t\\end{itemize}\n617\t\n618\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n619\t\\centering\n620\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/overview_setting_and_method.png}\n621\t\\caption{Setting and compared constructions. With labels, unlabeled records and the predictor fixed, three intervals are built from the same data: the labeled-only interval, PPI++ (scalar weight on $\\Yhat$), and recalibrated PPI (cross-fitted $g(\\Yhat)$ as an additional control variate, with a fallback to PPI++). Each is scored by empirical coverage and by the width ratio to PPI++; the achievable narrowing is bounded by the drop from $\\Var(Y-\\lambda\\Yhat)$ to the residual variance of the best $g$.}\n622\t\\label{fig:overview}\n623\t\\end{figure}\n624\t\n625\t\\section{Related Work}\n626\t\\paragraph{Prediction-powered inference.} PPI \\citep{Angelopoulos2023PredictionpoweredI} corrects a prediction-based estimate with a rectifier measured on labeled data; PPI++ \\citep{Angelopoulos2023PPIEP} tunes a scalar weight to minimize the interval width and is our primary comparator. Cross-prediction-powered inference \\citep{Zrnic2023CrosspredictionpoweredI} handles predictors trained on the same labeled data by cross-fitting, a device we reuse for the recalibration map. Stratified PPI \\citep{Fisch2024StratifiedPI} corrects within strata of the predictor output; we include an implementation as a reference estimator but report no results for it. Further variants cover imputed covariates and nonuniform sampling \\citep{Kluger2025PredictionPoweredIW}, e-values \\citep{Csillag2025PredictionPoweredE}, Bayesian and Bayes-assisted constructions \\citep{Hofer2024BayesianPI,Kilian2025AnytimevalidBI,Cortinovis2025FABPPIFA}, adaptive shrinkage \\citep{Li2025PredictionPoweredAS}, local and sparse regression settings \\citep{Gu2024LocalPI,Song2025SparseEA}, federated data \\citep{Luo2024FederatedPI}, causal estimands \\citep{Cadei2025PredictionPoweredCI}, risk control \\citep{Einbinder2024SemiSupervisedRC}, clinical trials \\citep{Poulet2025PredictionpoweredIF}, wireless systems \\citep{Sifaou2024SemiSupervisedLV} and satellite-based poverty analysis \\citep{Pettersson2025DebiasingML}. These works change the estimand, the sampling design or the inferential framework; we instead ask how much a plain mean interval can still be shortened with the inputs fixed.\n627\t\n628\t\\paragraph{Semi-supervised and debiased estimation.} The control-variate form of PPI has antecedents in the classical use of control variables to reduce estimator variance \\citep{Lavenberg1981Perspective}, in augmented inverse-probability-weighted estimation with missing outcomes \\citep{Robins1994Estimation,Bang2005Doubly}, in semi-supervised mean estimation \\citep{Zhang2016SemisupervisedIG} and in semiparametrically efficient semi-supervised learning \\citep{Xu2025AUF}, where efficiency is characterized through the conditional mean of the outcome. Our headroom formula (Section~\\ref{sec:theory}) is a finite-$n/N$ restatement of this efficiency logic for the case where the only available function of the data is $\\Yhat$; we do not claim it as a new efficiency result, and we did not verify it against these sources beyond the qualitative correspondence. Debiased machine learning with cross-fitted nuisance estimates \\citep{Chernozhukov2017DoubledebiasedML} supplies the template for using an estimated conditional mean inside a valid interval. Our setting differs in that the covariates are not available and the only usable function of the data is $\\Yhat$.\n629\t\n630\t\\paragraph{Calibration.} Isotonic and binned maps from scores to outcome probabilities are standard calibration tools \\citep{Zadrozny2002TransformingCS,Niculescu-Mizil2005PredictingGP}. Isotonic regression also extends beyond probabilities to monotone conditional-distribution estimation for general real-valued outcomes \\citep{Henzi2021Isotonic}. Here the maps estimate $\\E[Y\\mid \\Yhat]$ for use as a control variate, not to produce probabilities.\n631\t\n632\t\\section{Method}\\label{sec:method}\n633\t\\subsection{Problem setup}\n634\tLet $(Y_i,\\Yhat_i)$, $i=1,\\dots,n$ be labeled pairs and $\\Yhat_j$, $j=1,\\dots,N$ unlabeled predictions, all i.i.d.\\ from one population, with a fixed predictor producing $\\Yhat$. The target is $\\theta=\\E[Y]$, the mean estimand, and the interval must have coverage at least $1-\\alpha$ with $\\alpha=0.05$ under repeated sampling. We write $r=n/N$. The objective is the interval width at maintained coverage. No additional labels, data or predictor changes are allowed, and no covariates enter beyond $\\Yhat$.\n635\t\n636\t\\subsection{Control-variate view and headroom}\\label{sec:theory}\n637\tFor a feature vector $f(\\Yhat)\\in\\mathbb{R}^p$ and weights $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb{R}^p$ define\n638\t\\begin{equation}\n639\t\\hat\\theta_\\lambda=\\frac1N\\sum_{j=1}^N\\lambda^\\top f(\\Yhat_j)+\\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n\\bigl(Y_i-\\lambda^\\top f(\\Yhat_i)\\bigr),\n640\t\\end{equation}\n641\twhich is unbiased for fixed $\\lambda$ and $f$. Its asymptotic variance satisfies $n\\,V(\\lambda)=\\Var(Y)-2\\lambda^\\top c+(1+r)\\lambda^\\top\\Sigma\\lambda$ with $c=\\Cov(f,Y)$ and $\\Sigma=\\Var(f)$, minimized at $\\lambda^\\star=\\Sigma^{-1}c/(1+r)$:\n642\t\\begin{equation}\n643\tn\\,V^\\star=\\Var(Y)\\Bigl(1-\\frac{R_f^2}{1+r}\\Bigr),\\qquad R_f^2=\\frac{c^\\top\\Sigma^{-1}c}{\\Var(Y)}.\n644\t\\label{eq:vstar}\n645\t\\end{equation}\n646\tHere $f=0$ gives the labeled-only interval, $f=\\Yhat$ with $\\lambda=1$ gives PPI, and $f=\\Yhat$ with the estimated weight clipped to $[0,1]$ gives PPI++ (the clip is applied in the reference implementation we compare against; Supplement~\\ref{sec:si-source}). With an unclipped weight on $\\Yhat$ the explained share is $R_f^2=\\rho^2$, the squared Pearson correlation. If the feature set contains $m(\\Yhat)=\\E[Y\\mid\\Yhat]$ then $R_f^2=\\eta^2:=\\Var(m(\\Yhat))/\\Var(Y)$, the correlation ratio. Hence the oracle standard-error ratio between a recalibrated and an unclipped-linear interval is\n647\t\\begin{equation}\n648\t\\sqrt{\\frac{1-\\eta^2/(1+r)}{1-\\rho^2/(1+r)}},\\qquad\\text{which tends to }\\sqrt{\\frac{1-\\eta^2}{1-\\rho^2}}\\text{ as }r\\to0.\n649\t\\label{eq:ceiling}\n650\t\\end{equation}\n651\tTwo things can therefore shorten a PPI++ interval with fixed inputs: allowing the weight to leave $[0,1]$ when the unclipped optimum $\\lambda^\\star>1$, and capturing nonlinearity of $\\E[Y\\mid\\Yhat]$, measured by $\\eta^2-\\rho^2\\ge0$. The gain is \\emph{zero} when (i) $\\E[Y\\mid\\Yhat]$ is affine in $\\Yhat$, which includes every predictor taking only two values, since two points are always collinear (this removes the nonlinearity term only; the total gain is then zero provided the unclipped optimal weight also lies in $[0,1]$); (ii) $\\Yhat$ is uninformative ($\\rho=\\eta=0$); or (iii) $n$ is small enough that estimating $g$ costs more variance than it removes, a loss of order $p/n$ in relative variance. These are asymptotic statements for fixed $g$; with a cross-fitted $\\hat g$ they require that $\\hat g$ converge to $m$ at an adequate rate, which we assume and do not prove here (derivation in Supplement~\\ref{sec:si-deriv}).\n652\t\n653\t\\subsection{Compared estimators}\n654\tTable~\\ref{tab:estimators} lists the interval constructions. All use $\\alpha=0.05$ and an estimated standard error from the labeled residual and the unlabeled control-variate term.\n655\t\n656\t\\begin{table}[t]\n657\t\\centering\n658\t\\caption{Interval constructions compared. ``Weight'' is the control-variate weight on the listed features.}\n659\t\\label{tab:estimators}\n660\t\\small\n661\t\\begin{tabular}{p{2.6cm}p{2.9cm}p{3.1cm}p{3.6cm}}\n662\t\\toprule\n663\tInterval & Features & Weight & Critical value / notes \\\\\n664\t\\midrule\n665\tLabeled-only & none & --- & normal; uses labels only \\\\\n666\tPPI & $\\Yhat$ & $\\lambda{=}1$ & normal \\\\\n667\tPPI++ & $\\Yhat$ & scalar, estimated, clipped to $[0,1]$ & normal; primary comparator, also the fallback \\\\\n668\tStratified PPI & quantile strata of $\\Yhat$ & $\\lambda{=}1$ per stratum & normal; implemented, no reported results \\\\\n669\tRecalibrated PPI (studied) & $\\Yhat$ and cross-fitted $g(\\Yhat)$ & vector, estimated, unclipped & Student-$t$, $n{-}1{-}p$ d.f.; adopted only if $\\ge1\\%$ shorter than PPI++ \\\\\n670\t\\bottomrule\n671\t\\end{tabular}\n672\t\\end{table}\n673\t\n674\t\\subsection{Cross-fitted recalibrated PPI}\n675\tLet $K=5$. Labeled indices are split at random into $K$ folds. For each fold, a recalibrator $g^{(k)}$ is fitted on the other folds. Out-of-fold values $g^{(k)}(\\Yhat_i)$ are used on the held-out labeled points, and the average $K^{-1}\\sum_k g^{(k)}(\\Yhat_j)$ is used on the unlabeled points. Two recalibrators are used: an isotonic fit whose direction follows the sign of the sample correlation, and a quantile-binned conditional mean with $\\min(10,\\max(2,\\lfloor 0.8n/25\\rfloor))$ bins. The two features $(\\Yhat,g)$ are standardized with their pooled mean and standard deviation, and the weight solves $\\lambda=(\\hat\\Sigma_\\ell+r\\hat\\Sigma_u+\\epsilon I)^{-1}\\hat c$ with a small ridge $\\epsilon$. The interval is the control-variate estimate plus or minus a Student-$t$ quantile with $n-1-p$ degrees of freedom ($p=2$) times $\\sqrt{s_u^2/N+s_\\ell^2/n}$, where $s_\\ell^2$ is the residual variance on labeled points and $s_u^2$ the variance of the weighted control variate on unlabeled points.\n676\t\n677\t\\paragraph{Selection with fallback.} The PPI++ interval is computed first. A recalibrated candidate is adopted only if its half-width is below 0.99 times the PPI++ half-width. The isotonic candidate is tried first and the binned candidate second; once the isotonic candidate has been adopted, the binned candidate replaces it if it is merely narrower, with no additional margin. If neither candidate passes the test against PPI++, PPI++ is returned. If $n<20$ or $\\Yhat$ is constant, no candidate is formed. The 1\\% margin is a heuristic guard against selection noise, not a validity guarantee; the interval chosen by comparing estimated widths is data-dependent, and the standard-error estimate does not account for the estimation of $g$ or of $\\lambda$ beyond the degrees-of-freedom correction.\n678\t\n679\t\\subsection{Evaluation protocol}\n680\t\\paragraph{Pools and draws.} The sealed evaluation uses four real pools, each with $n=500$ labeled and $N=20000$ unlabeled records: California census income, census private-insurance indicator, a census education-related quantity (all with model predictions; census tabular benchmarks of the kind assembled by \\citealt{Ding2021RetiringAN}), and a plankton-imaging pool whose prediction takes only two values. A fifth family is synthetic with four regimes (linear calibrated, nonlinear miscalibrated with a cubic predictor, uninformative, heteroscedastic), $n\\in\\{200,500\\}$ and $N\\in\\{2000,20000\\}$. Each family has 100 independent draws; real-pool draws are taken with replacement from the pool, so the truth is the pool mean, and synthetic truths are analytic. Controls with a constant predictor, a permuted predictor and a shifted outcome are run separately.\n681\t\n682\t\\paragraph{Score and validity gate.} For each family $f$ the evaluator forms $s_f=-\\log(\\sum_e W_e/\\sum_e P_e)$ from candidate widths $W_e$ and PPI++ widths $P_e$ over its episodes (positive means narrower), and averages $s_f$ over the five families. The same construction against PPI ($\\lambda{=}1$) and the labeled-only interval gives the other two comparators; baselines come from ppi-python 0.2.3. Validity requires non-rejection of nominal coverage by exact one-sided binomial tests (pooled level 0.01, per-family level $0.01/5$, failed episodes counted as misses with worst-case width) and all controls passing. A paired episode-level bootstrap of the aggregate score (2000 replicates) must have a positive 2.5th percentile for the narrowing to be declared. Non-rejection is a weaker statement than coverage equal to nominal.\n683\t\n684\t\\paragraph{Design-space contrast.} Before the sealed evaluation, three model variants were scored once each on separate search pools (galaxy morphology, Amazon deforestation, gene expression, protein disorder, plus synthetic regimes; 60 draws per family): the recalibrated interval above, a label-free spline-basis control variate with leave-one-out PRESS model selection, and a repeated-cross-fitted calibrator stack combined by non-negative least squares. These are single evaluations on search pools and are reported as a contrast in Section~\\ref{sec:search}, not as controlled ablations; the search pools are disjoint from the sealed pools.\n685\t\n686\t\\section{Results}\\label{sec:results}\n687\t\\subsection{Validity: coverage evidence}\\label{sec:validity}\n688\tThe sealed evaluation completed with all controls passed, zero failed episodes, and the validity gate and the bootstrap criterion satisfied (final outcome \\emph{qualified}). Pooled empirical coverage over the 500 evaluated draws is 0.934 (467 covered) and the lowest family coverage is 0.91 (91 of 100). \n689\tBoth point estimates lie below the nominal 0.95. Under exact binomial calculations with $p=0.95$, the lower-tail probabilities of the observed counts are 0.066 (pooled) and 0.063 (lowest family), \n690\twhich is why the gate did not reject, but also not strong support for exact nominal coverage; the Monte Carlo standard errors are 0.011 and 0.029. \n691\tCoverage of the PPI++ and labeled-only baselines was not recorded in the available artifacts, and per-family coverage other than the minimum is unavailable. We therefore cannot say whether the shortfall is a property of recalibration, of finite-sample Wald intervals at these sample sizes, or of the data-dependent selection step."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"64cbee98-e155-4c0c-b11f-e1ce1b3430d6","uuid":"385681a3-905d-413b-afe7-26cec6a05a58","timestamp":"2026-11-11T12:36:27.497Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/paper_evidence_audit_packet.md","content":"# Agentic evidence audit packet\n\n- generated_at: `2026-11-11T12:36:22.812706+00:00`\n- paper_dir: `work/results/t10/claude/20261111_094019/.paper_evidence_audit_workspaces-1000/agentic-evidence-audit-1000-20261111_114006-paper_evidence_audit_round_6-py0ux_y_/workspace`\n\nUse this packet as the default entrypoint. Read raw artifacts only when this packet is insufficient.\nThe goal is narrow: check whether the paper's visible claims remain consistent with claims_grounding.json, paper_data.json, and the scientific-evidence trail.\n\n## Expected audit focus\n\n- visible claims in `paper.tex` that overstate, contradict, or drift from `claims_grounding.json`\n- claims grounded to the wrong source ids, or source ids that do not support the visible wording\n- mismatches between visible manuscript text and the resolved/anchored evidence trail\n- cases where the evidence trail exists but does not justify the paper's wording or numeric precision\n- contradictions between `paper.tex`, `claims_grounding.json`, `paper_data.json`, and `scientific-evidence/verification-report.json`\n- every visible claim in Supplementary Information, including tables and figures, under the same grounding standard as the main paper\n\n## Artifact checklist\n\n- `paper.tex` — `present`\n- `claims_grounding.json` — `present`\n- `paper_data.json` — `present`\n- `validation_report.json` — `present`\n- `scientific-evidence/verification-report.json` — `prese