{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XzyGDpzsNbg3BqVQ3cp7yn","type":"tool_result","content":"150\t\\small\n151\t\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrr}\n152\t\\toprule\n153\tFamily & Observed & Reduction & Ceiling & Unclipped-linear part & Observed $-$ ceiling \\\\\n154\t\\midrule\n155\tcensus income & 0.0287 & 2.8\\% & 0.0213 & 0.0179 & 0.0074 \\\\\n156\tcensus insurance & 0.0018 & 0.2\\% & 0.0008 & 0.0000 & 0.0009 \\\\\n157\tcensus education & 0.0023 & 0.2\\% & 0.0009 & 0.0000 & 0.0014 \\\\\n158\tplankton & 0.0359 & 3.5\\% & 0.0000 & 0.0000 & 0.0359 \\\\\n159\tsynthetic & 0.0413 & 4.0\\% & 0.0416 & 0.0000 & $-$0.0003 \\\\\n160\t\\midrule\n161\taggregate & 0.0220 & 2.2\\% & 0.0129 & 0.0036 & 0.0091 \\\\\n162\t\\bottomrule\n163\t\\end{tabular}\n164\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics]] @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#oracle_vs_ppi_pp]] @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#oracle_linear_vs_ppi_pp]]\n165\t\\end{table}\n166\t\n167\t\\subsection{How does the observed gain compare with the population-level ceiling?}\\label{sec:headroomcheck}\n168\tWe evaluated Eq.~\\eqref{eq:ceiling} on each pool, using $\\rho^2$ and an isotonic estimate of $\\eta^2$ on the full pool, $n=500$, $N=20000$, and the evaluator's PPI++ weight clip (for synthetic regimes, large-sample moments of the generator and the evaluator's episode plan). The population ceiling is not a bound on a single finite draw, and it excludes the estimation cost of $g$.\n169\t\n170\t\\emph{Where the ceiling is positive, it is small.} The ceiling against PPI++ is 0.0213 for census income, 0.0008 and 0.0009 for the two other census pools, and 0.0416 for the synthetic mixture; the aggregate is 0.0129. % @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#oracle_vs_ppi_pp]] @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#oracle_aggregate_vs_ppi_pp]]\n171\tFor census income, the unclipped optimal weight computed from the pool is 1.34 (the clipped weight equals 1), and 0.0179 of the 0.0213 ceiling is reachable by an unclipped linear weight on $\\Yhat$ alone; the pool's $\\eta^2$ and $\\rho^2$ differ by only 0.0044. % @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#oracle_linear_vs_ppi_pp]] @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#real.census_income]]\n172\tSo for this pool the population-level ceiling is mostly reachable by an unclipped linear weight rather than by nonlinear recalibration; this describes the ceiling, not the source of the evaluated interval's observed gain, which was not isolated. For the synthetic family, the entire ceiling comes from the nonlinear-miscalibrated regime ($\\eta^2=0.71$ against $\\rho^2=0.48$), and the observed 0.0413 matches the ceiling 0.0416 within 0.0003. % @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#synthetic_regimes]]\n173\t\n174\t\\emph{Where the observed gain exceeds the ceiling.} In four of five families the observed narrowing is larger than the ceiling (Table~\\ref{tab:final}). The plankton pool is the clearest case: the predictor takes only two values, so $\\E[Y\\mid\\Yhat]$ is affine in $\\Yhat$, the unclipped optimal weight (0.841) lies inside $[0,1]$, the ceiling is zero, yet the observed narrowing is 0.0359 (3.5\\%). % @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#real.plankton]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.log_ratio_vs_ppi_family_plankton]]\n175\tThe aggregate observed value 0.0220 exceeds the aggregate ceiling 0.0129 by 0.0091. The same holds against PPI with $\\lambda{=}1$ (0.0701 observed, 0.0661 ceiling) and against the labeled-only interval (0.3581 observed, 0.3498 ceiling). % @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#oracle_aggregate_vs_ppi_lam1]] @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#oracle_aggregate_vs_classical]]\n176\tAsymptotic variance reduction from recalibration cannot account for this part. We did not run a diagnostic that isolates its source. Candidate explanations, none tested here, are (a) finite-sample optimism of an interval whose weight and residual variance are estimated on the same labeled data, (b) selection of the narrowest of three estimated widths (PPI++ and the isotonic and binned candidates, with a 1\\% margin required only relative to PPI++), which favors downward noise in the estimated standard error, and (c) differences in finite-sample variance estimation between the evaluated implementation and the reference baseline. Explanations (a) and (b) would also lower coverage and are consistent with the coverage shortfall of Section~\\ref{sec:validity}, but the available artifacts do not allow us to establish that link. For the two census pools with near-zero ceilings, the excess is 0.0009 and 0.0014; with no per-episode widths in the artifacts, we cannot tell whether it is distinguishable from pairing noise.\n177\t\n178\t\\emph{Regime-level headroom is much larger than the family-level average.} Within the nonlinear-miscalibrated synthetic regime alone, the same formula gives an oracle narrowing over an unclipped linear weight of 0.286 in log width for $(n,N)=(200,20000)$ and 0.178 for $(500,2000)$ (Table~\\ref{tab:rsens}), i.e.\\ roughly 25\\% to 16\\% shorter intervals; the gain falls as $r=n/N$ grows because the unlabeled sample then carries less of the variance. The family-level ceiling of 0.0416 is much smaller because three of the four regimes have no headroom and the evaluator's ratio of summed widths weights the nonlinear regime by its (smallest) interval width. For census income the same calculation gives at most 0.0034 over an unclipped linear weight, whatever $r$. % @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#oracle_recal_over_linear_by_cell]]\n179\t\n180\t\\begin{table}[t]\n181\t\\centering\n182\t\\caption{Oracle recalibration gain over an unclipped linear weight on $\\hat Y$ (log width ratio, Eq.~\\eqref{eq:ceiling}) by sample-size cell, for the one synthetic regime with nonlinear calibration and for the census-income pool (derived).}\n183\t\\label{tab:rsens}\n184\t\\small\n185\t\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n186\t\\toprule\n187\t & $(200,20000)$ & $(500,20000)$ & $(200,2000)$ & $(500,2000)$ \\\\\n188\t$r=n/N$ & 0.010 & 0.025 & 0.100 & 0.250 \\\\\n189\t\\midrule\n190\tsynthetic, nonlinear regime & 0.2855 & 0.2750 & 0.2323 & 0.1776 \\\\\n191\tcensus income & 0.0034 & 0.0034 & 0.0030 & 0.0025 \\\\\n192\t\\bottomrule\n193\t\\end{tabular}\n194\t% @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#oracle_recal_over_linear_by_cell]]\n195\t\\end{table}\n196\t\n197\t\\subsection{Stability across evaluator partitions}\n198\tThe evaluator also reports the aggregate score separately for five partitions of the episodes, defined by the candidate reset seed: 0.0247, 0.0220, 0.0203, 0.0213 and 0.0235, all positive. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n199\tThese values are a spread, not a confidence interval: the data draws do not depend on the reset seed, and because of the evaluator's episode ordering each partition contains 76 episodes of one family and six of each other family, so partitions differ in composition (Supplement~\\ref{sec:si-partition}). % @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#partition_composition_main_episodes]]\n200\t\n201\t\\subsection{Design-space contrast on the search pools}\\label{sec:search}\n202\tFigure~\\ref{fig:search} compares the three model variants, each evaluated once on the search pools. Their aggregate scores against PPI++ are 0.0271 (recalibrated isotonic/binned), 0.0223 (stacked calibration) and 0.0046 (label-free basis with PRESS); all three were scored and passed the evaluator's validity gate. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#grounding_sources.result.top_program_01]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#grounding_sources.result.top_program_02]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#grounding_sources.result.top_program_03]]\n203\tThe recalibrated variant is largest on the synthetic family (0.0757) and gene expression (0.0290), and smallest on galaxy morphology (0.0035). % @evidence[[source_evidence/search_metrics/recalibrated_metrics.json]]\n204\tThe stacked variant tracks it on the synthetic family (0.0707) and gene expression (0.0278) but is smaller on deforestation and protein disorder; the smooth label-free basis is close to PPI++ on the synthetic family (0.0034), where the nonlinearity is the exponential regime of the search generator. % @evidence[[source_evidence/search_metrics/stacked_metrics.json]] @evidence[[source_evidence/search_metrics/label_free_basis_metrics.json]]\n205\tSearch-pool pooled coverage was 0.943 for the recalibrated and the stacked variants and 0.950 for the basis variant, with minimum family coverage 0.933, 0.917 and 0.933 respectively. % @evidence[[source_evidence/search_metrics/recalibrated_metrics.json]] @evidence[[source_evidence/search_metrics/stacked_metrics.json]] @evidence[[source_evidence/search_metrics/label_free_basis_metrics.json]]\n206\tThese are single evaluations without uncertainty estimates, and the variants differ in several components at once (recalibrator class, variance estimator, selection rule), so the contrast cannot attribute the difference to any one component. The stacked variant scored lower than the simpler recalibrated variant on the aggregate (0.0223 against 0.0271). We computed no headroom ceilings for the search pools, so we offer no explanation of this difference.\n207\t\n208\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n209\t\\centering\n210\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/search_variant_contrast.pdf}\n211\t\\caption{Design-space contrast on the search pools: log width ratio against PPI++ for three model variants, five families, one evaluation per variant. Separate from, and not comparable in scale to, the sealed final results.}\n212\t\\label{fig:search}\n213\t\\end{figure}\n214\t\n215\t\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\\label{sec:discussion}\n216\t\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} With fixed inputs, an interval narrower than PPI++ exists in the families we tested, but the average margin is about 2\\% in width and the margin depends strongly on the pool: close to zero for the two other census pools (insurance, education) and a few percent for census income and the synthetic mixture. These margins come from the whole evaluated configuration (cross-fitted recalibration, Student-$t$ quantile, variance estimator and PPI++ fallback), none of whose components was ablated, so we do not attribute them to any single component. The population-level diagnostic is of the same sign and roughly the same size as the observed margin for the synthetic mixture and, less closely, for census income, but it predicts none for the plankton pool (a two-valued predictor whose unclipped optimal weight lies inside $[0,1]$), where the observed margin is 3.5\\%, and it does not account for the observed gain in four of five families.\n217\t\n218\t\\paragraph{What it does not support.} The evidence does not show that the narrowing is free: coverage point estimates are 0.934 pooled and 0.91 for the lowest family, below 0.95, and the baselines' coverage is unknown. The observed gain exceeds the ceiling in four of five families, so part of it is not accounted for by the population-level diagnostic, and for plankton in particular the gain cannot be attributed to nonlinear calibration. The paper does not claim superiority beyond the pools evaluated, validity under the selection rule beyond the empirical gate, or generalization to other estimands or to covariate shift.\n219\t\n220\t\\paragraph{Limitations.} (1) Estimand: only the population mean at $\\alpha=0.05$. (2) Sample sizes: $n=500$ on real pools and $n\\in\\{200,500\\}$ on synthetic draws; no sweep over $n$ or $N$, although the theory predicts that small $n$ erodes the gain. (3) Single sealed run, no confidence intervals on width ratios, no per-episode widths or per-family coverage beyond the minimum in the artifacts, and no controlled ablation of the recalibrator, the second control variate, the Student-$t$ quantile or the selection rule. The mechanisms discussed above are therefore hypotheses. (4) The ceiling is a population-level asymptotic quantity computed with the evaluator's finite pools as the population; it is a diagnostic, not a bound on a finite draw. (5) The truth for real pools is the pool mean, with draws from the pool with replacement, so the evaluation checks inference for that finite population rather than for the original scientific population. (6) The evaluator's diagnostics for the candidate are marked as unverified. (7) The selection rule makes the interval data-dependent and the 1\\% margin has no theoretical guarantee. (8) Stratified PPI is implemented but has no reported metrics and is not compared numerically.\n221\t\n222\t\\paragraph{Experiments that would test the hypotheses.} The mechanisms above can be tested with controlled comparisons that we did not run: (i) PPI++ with the weight clip removed, to separate the clipping component from nonlinear calibration; (ii) the recalibrated interval with the fallback disabled and with a fixed choice of recalibrator, to separate selection effects from the second control variate; (iii) the Student-$t$ quantile replaced by the normal quantile; (iv) coverage and width of every baseline, computed on the same draws and reported per family; and (v) a grid over $n$ and $N$ on the real pools, where the theory predicts a gain that shrinks with $n$ (estimation cost of order $p/n$) and with $r$. Until these are available, the decomposition in Section~\\ref{sec:headroomcheck} should be read as a diagnostic of what is possible, not as an attribution of what the evaluated interval does.\n223\t\n224\t\\paragraph{Practical reading.} As a population-level computation, for census income about 84\\% of the ceiling against PPI++ (0.0179 of 0.0213) is reachable by an unclipped linear weight on $\\Yhat$, whereas in the synthetic nonlinear regime the ceiling arises from the nonlinearity of $\\E[Y\\mid\\Yhat]$. This describes what is possible in the population, not what the evaluated interval does. Whether an unclipped PPI++ interval attains it, and at what coverage cost, was not tested here.\n225\t\n226\t\\section{Conclusion}\n227\tWith labels, unlabeled data and predictor held fixed, the evaluated configuration (cross-fitted recalibration as a second control variate, Student-$t$ quantile and a PPI++ fallback) gave mean intervals 2.2\\% narrower than PPI++ on average in a sealed evaluation, with reductions between 0.2\\% and 4.0\\% by family, and 6.8\\% and 30\\% narrower than PPI with $\\lambda{=}1$ and the labeled-only interval. No component was ablated, so the gain is not attributed to recalibration. A closed-form population-level headroom diagnostic is small (aggregate 0.0129) and regime-dependent, and matches the observed margin closely only for the synthetic mixture and roughly for census income; it is zero for the plankton pool and does not account for the observed gain in four of five families. Coverage point estimates are 0.934 pooled and 0.91 for the lowest family, below the nominal 0.95, although the evaluator's tests did not reject. We read the result as conditional support for a small gain, not as evidence that PPI++ intervals can be shortened for free. The most useful next experiments are a coverage and width comparison of an unclipped PPI++ weight and each recalibration component in isolation, per-episode width and coverage reporting for all baselines, and a sweep over $n$.\n228\t\n229\t% IMPORTANT: \\clearpage flushes all pending floats before references so that\n230\t% body-page counting excludes reference-only pages.  Do NOT remove.\n231\t\\clearpage\n232\t\\bibliography{references}\n233\t\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n234\t\n235\t\\clearpage\n236\t\\appendix\n237\t\\section{Supplementary Information}\n238\t\n239\t\\subsection{Source evidence and implementation details}\\label{sec:si-source}\n240\tImplementation statements in this paper refer to the readable files in \\texttt{source\\_evidence/}: \\texttt{frozen\\_winner\\_estimator.py} (the recalibrated interval), \\texttt{variant\\_label\\_free\\_basis\\_estimator.py} and \\texttt{variant\\_stacked\\_calibration\\_estimator.py} (the two contrast variants), \\texttt{evaluate\\_final.py} and \\texttt{evaluate\\_search.py} (the two evaluators; the first has the SHA-256 recorded by the sealed evaluation), and \\texttt{ppi\\_py\\_0.2.3\\_excerpt.txt} (the reference PPI++ weight, which is clipped to $[0,1]$ when \\texttt{clip=True}, the value used by the default mean interval). We assume the frozen file is the one evaluated in the sealed run, as recorded by the run manifest; hashes alone do not establish it.\n241\t\n242\tSettings of the evaluated interval: $K=5$ shuffled folds seeded from the candidate's random generator; isotonic fit with clipped out-of-bounds prediction; binned fit with $\\min(10,\\max(2,\\lfloor 0.8n/25\\rfloor))$ bins (10 for $n=500$, 6 for $n=200$); pooled standardization of features; ridge $10^{-6}\\,\\mathrm{tr}(A)/p$ on the weight system; Student-$t$ quantile via a Cornish--Fisher expansion with degrees of freedom floored at 3; PPI++ weight clipped to $[0,1]$; fallback margin 0.99 applied to the PPI++ half-width only (the narrower of the isotonic and binned candidates is kept, with no margin between them). The evaluator enforces a 3\\,s per-estimate deadline on the evaluated estimator.\n243\t\n244\t\\paragraph{Contrast variants.} The label-free basis variant uses a continuous piecewise-linear basis of $\\Yhat$ with knots at pooled quantiles, the model size $p\\in\\{1,2,3,5,8\\}$ is chosen by leave-one-out PRESS with a 2\\% improvement margin, the standard error uses leverage-corrected leave-one-out residuals, and a guard keeps the width ratio against PPI++ above 0.85. The stacked variant fits seven calibrators (isotonic, three binned with 3, 5 and 8 bins, monotone and local-linear kernels, linear), combines them by non-negative least squares with 4 inner folds, repeats $K=5$ cross-fitting $R=5$ times under a time budget, and uses averaged-residual variance plus a split-dispersion term with $n-3$ degrees of freedom.\n245\t\n246\t\\subsection{Variance derivation}\\label{sec:si-deriv}\n247\tFor fixed $f,\\lambda$, the two terms of $\\hat\\theta_\\lambda$ are independent, so $V(\\lambda)=\\Var(Y-\\lambda^\\top f)/n+\\Var(\\lambda^\\top f)/N$. Expanding, $nV(\\lambda)=\\Var(Y)-2\\lambda^\\top c+\\lambda^\\top\\Sigma\\lambda+r\\lambda^\\top\\Sigma\\lambda$, a quadratic with minimizer $\\lambda^\\star=\\Sigma^{-1}c/(1+r)$ and minimum $\\Var(Y)-c^\\top\\Sigma^{-1}c/(1+r)$. For $f=\\Yhat$, $c^\\top\\Sigma^{-1}c=\\Cov(Y,\\Yhat)^2/\\Var(\\Yhat)=\\rho^2\\Var(Y)$. For $f=(\\Yhat,m(\\Yhat))$ the linear span contains $m$, and the squared multiple correlation of $Y$ on the span equals $\\eta^2$. The PPI++ weight with its clip uses $\\lambda=\\mathrm{clip}(\\Cov(Y,\\Yhat)/((1+r)\\Var(\\Yhat)),0,1)$, whose variance is the quadratic evaluated at that point. These formulas are what the headroom script evaluates (\\texttt{analysis/headroom.py}).\n248\t\n249\t\\subsection{Evaluator partitions}\\label{sec:si-partition}\n250\tFigure~\\ref{fig:partition} shows the five partition scores. Replicating the evaluator's episode plan shows that, after the first 30 draws, every family keeps the same reset seed, so each partition holds 76 draws of one family and 6 of every other. The scores mix large and very small per-family samples and should be read as a rough spread only.\n251\t\n252\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n253\t\\centering\n254\t\\includegraphics[width=0.62\\textwidth]{figures/posthoc/headroom_partition_scores/partition_scores.pdf}\n255\t\\caption{Aggregate log width ratio against PPI++ for each evaluator partition (candidate reset seed), with the pooled aggregate (dashed). Not independent replications.}\n256\t\\label{fig:partition}\n257\t\\end{figure}\n258\t\n259\t\\subsection{Search-pool metrics and sealed-evaluation details}\n260\t\\begin{table}[h]\n261\t\\centering\n262\t\\caption{Search-pool metrics for the three model variants (single evaluation each): log width ratio against PPI++ by family, aggregate score, and coverage.}\n263\t\\label{tab:search}\n264\t\\small\n265\t\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrrrrr}\n266\t\\toprule\n267\tVariant & galax. & forest & gene & alpha. & synth. & agg. & cov.\\ pooled & cov.\\ min \\\\\n268\t\\midrule\n269\trecalibrated & 0.0035 & 0.0168 & 0.0290 & 0.0104 & 0.0757 & 0.0271 & 0.943 & 0.933 \\\\\n270\tstacked & 0.0036 & 0.0054 & 0.0278 & 0.0038 & 0.0707 & 0.0223 & 0.943 & 0.917 \\\\\n271\tlabel-free basis & 0.0014 & 0.0024 & 0.0111 & 0.0049 & 0.0034 & 0.0046 & 0.950 & 0.933 \\\\\n272\t\\bottomrule\n273\t\\end{tabular}\n274\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/search_metrics/recalibrated_metrics.json]] @evidence[[source_evidence/search_metrics/stacked_metrics.json]] @evidence[[source_evidence/search_metrics/label_free_basis_metrics.json]]\n275\t\\end{table}\n276\t\n277\tSearch pools: galaxy morphology (Galaxy Zoo-type crowd labels, \\citealt{Willett2013GalaxyZ2}; $n=300$, $N=5000$), Amazon deforestation ($n=100$, $N=1000$), gene expression ($n=500$, $N=10000$), protein disorder ($n=300$, $N=5000$), and synthetic regimes, 60 draws per family, reset seeds 1001--1005. We cite an original data source only for the galaxy and census pools; the original sources of the plankton, deforestation, gene-expression and protein-disorder pools were not traced for this paper. The sealed evaluation plan declares 100 draws for each of five families (500 main episodes) plus 30 draws for each of two base pools and three control types (180 control episodes), 680 in total (constants \\texttt{REPS\\_MAIN} and \\texttt{REPS\\_CONTROL} in \\texttt{source\\_evidence/evaluate\\_final.py}). The five recorded partitions have durations that sum to about 1332 seconds, against a wall-clock limit of 5400 seconds in the same script. % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n278\t\n279\t\\paragraph{Population quantities for the headroom check.} Table~\\ref{tab:moments} lists the squared correlation and the correlation ratio per family.\n280\t\n281\t\\begin{table}[h]\n282\t\\centering\n283\t\\caption{Pool-level moments used for the ceiling (isotonic estimate of $\\eta^2$; synthetic rows are large-sample regime moments). $\\lambda^\\star$ is the unclipped optimal weight on $\\Yhat$ at $n=500$, $N=20000$, not applicable (---) to the synthetic regimes, which mix several $(n,N)$ cells.}\n284\t\\label{tab:moments}\n285\t\\small\n286\t\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n287\t\\toprule\n288\tPool or regime & $\\rho^2$ & $\\eta^2$ & $\\eta^2-\\rho^2$ & $\\lambda^\\star$ \\\\\n289\t\\midrule\n290\tcensus income & 0.3691 & 0.3734 & 0.0044 & 1.342 \\\\\n291\tcensus insurance & 0.4996 & 0.5005 & 0.0009 & 0.961 \\\\\n292\tcensus education & 0.3921 & 0.3932 & 0.0011 & 0.953 \\\\\n293\tplankton (binary $\\Yhat$) & 0.7004 & 0.7004 & 0.0000 & 0.841 \\\\\n294\tsynthetic: linear calibrated & 0.5894 & 0.5895 & 0.0002 & --- \\\\\n295\tsynthetic: nonlinear miscalibrated & 0.4831 & 0.7124 & 0.2292 & --- \\\\\n296\tsynthetic: uninformative & 0.0000 & 0.0000 & 0.0000 & --- \\\\\n297\tsynthetic: heteroscedastic & 0.7614 & 0.7616 & 0.0002 & --- \\\\\n298\t\\bottomrule\n299\t\\end{tabular}\n300\t% @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#real]] @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#synthetic_regimes]]\n301\t\\end{table}\n302\t\n303\t\\subsection{Reproducibility}\n304\tThe derived quantities are produced by \\texttt{analysis/headroom.py} (reads the pool files, the evaluator's protocol constants and the sealed metrics, writes \\texttt{analysis/headroom\\_derived.json}) and the figures by \\texttt{analysis/make\\_figures.py}; both run in seconds on a CPU with NumPy, SciPy, scikit-learn and Matplotlib. Figure~\\ref{fig:overview} is a schematic with no data. Table~\\ref{tab:final} and Figure~\\ref{fig:final} come from the sealed metrics plus the headroom file, Table~\\ref{tab:search} and Figure~\\ref{fig:search} from the search-pool metrics files, and Figure~\\ref{fig:partition} from the sealed per-partition scores. The sealed evaluation itself is the external evaluator \\texttt{evaluate\\_final.py} run once on the frozen estimator, so it cannot be regenerated with different draws here. The synthetic ceilings use a fixed-seed Monte Carlo of $2\\times10^6$ draws per regime.\n305\t\n306\t\\subsection{LLM Usage}\n307\tA large language model agent system was used throughout this study. It proposed the estimator designs, wrote and debugged the estimator code, ran the search-pool evaluations, wrote the headroom analysis and figure scripts, and drafted this manuscript. The task definition, the data pools and both evaluators were provided externally and were not modified; the sealed evaluation was run by the evaluator on the frozen estimator. All numbers in this paper come from the evaluator outputs or from the scripts cited above.\n308\t\n309\t\\end{document}\n310\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"64cbee98-e155-4c0c-b11f-e1ce1b3430d6","uuid":"d06e1d10-7227-4a7f-9b61-1a8b92771f97","timestamp":"2026-11-11T12:36:33.353Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/paper.tex","content":"\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrr}\n\\toprule\nFamily & Observed & Reduction & Ceiling & Unclipped-linear part & Observed $-$ ceiling \\\\\n\\midrule\ncensus income & 0.0287 & 2.8\\% & 0.0213 & 0.0179 & 0.0074 \\\\\ncensus insurance & 0.0018 & 0.2\\% & 0.0008 & 0.0000 & 0.0009 \\\\\ncensus education & 0.0023 & 0.2\\% & 0.0009 & 0.0000 & 0.0014 \\\\\nplankton & 0.0359 & 3.5\\% & 0.0000 & 0.0000 & 0.0359 \\\\\nsynthetic & 0.0413 & 4.0\\% & 0.0416 & 0.0000 & $-$0.0003 \\\\\n\\midrule\naggregate & 0.0220 & 2.2\\% & 0.0129 & 0.0036 & 0.0091 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics]] @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#oracle_vs_ppi_pp]] @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#oracle_linear_vs_ppi_pp]]\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{How does the observed gain compare with the population-level ceiling?}\\label{sec:headroomcheck}\nWe evaluated Eq.~\\eqref{eq:ceiling} on each pool, using $\\rho^2$ and an isotonic estimate of $\\eta^2$ on the full pool, $n=500$, $N=20000$, and the evaluator's PPI++ weight clip (for synthetic regimes, large-sample moments of the generator and the evaluator's episode plan). The population ceiling is not a bound on a single finite draw, and it excludes the estimation cost of $g$.\n\n\\emph{Where the ceiling is positive, it is small.} The ceiling against PPI++ is 0.0213 for census income, 0.0008 and 0.0009 for the two other census pools, and 0.0416 for the synthetic mixture; the aggregate is 0.0129. % @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#oracle_vs_ppi_pp]] @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#oracle_aggregate_vs_ppi_pp]]\nFor census income, the unclipped optimal weight computed from the pool is 1.34 (the clipped weight equals 1), and 0.0179 of the 0.0213 ceiling is reachable by an unclipped linear weight on $\\Yhat$ alone; the pool's $\\eta^2$ and $\\rho^2$ differ by only 0.0044. % @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#oracle_linear_vs_ppi_pp]] @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#real.census_income]]\nSo for this pool the population-level ceiling is mostly reachable by an unclipped linear weight rather than by nonlinear recalibration; this describes the ceiling, not the source of the evaluated interval's observed gain, which was not isolated. For the synthetic family, the entire ceiling comes from the nonlinear-miscalibrated regime ($\\eta^2=0.71$ against $\\rho^2=0.48$), and the observed 0.0413 matches the ceiling 0.0416 within 0.0003. % @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#synthetic_regimes]]\n\n\\emph{Where the observed gain exceeds the ceiling.} In four of five families the observed narrowing is larger than the ceiling (Table~\\ref{tab:final}). The plankton pool is the clearest case: the predictor takes only two values, so $\\E[Y\\mid\\Yhat]$ is affine in $\\Yhat$, the unclipped optimal weight (0.841) lies inside $[0,1]$, the ceiling is zero, yet the observed narrowing is 0.0359 (3.5\\%). % @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#real.plankton]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.log_ratio_vs_ppi_family_plankton]]\nThe aggregate observed value 0.0220 exceeds the aggregate ceiling 0.0129 by 0.0091. The same holds against PPI with $\\lambda{=}1$ (0.0701 observed, 0.0661 ceiling) and against the labeled-only interval (0.3581 observed, 0.3498 ceiling). % @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#oracle_aggregate_vs_ppi_lam1]] @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#oracle_aggregate_vs_classical]]\nAsymptotic variance reduction from recalibration cannot account for this part. We did not run a diagnostic that isolates its source. Candidate explanations, none tested here, are (a) finite-sample optimism of an interval whose weight and residual variance are estimated on the same labeled data, (b) selection of the narrowest of three estimated widths (PPI++ and the isotonic and binned candidates, with a 1\\% margin required only relative to PPI++), which favors downward noise in the estimated standard error, and (c) differences in finite-sample variance estimation between the evaluated implementation and the reference baseline. Explanations (a) and (b) would also lower coverage and are consistent with the coverage shortfall of Section~\\ref{sec:validity}, but the available artifacts do not allow us to establish that link. For the two census pools with near-zero ceilings, the excess is 0.0009 and 0.0014; with no per-episode widths in the artifacts, we cannot tell whether it is distinguishable from pairing noise.\n\n\\emph{Regime-level headroom is much larger than the family-level average.} Within the nonlinear-miscalibrated synthetic regime alone, the same formula gives an oracle narrowing over an unclipped linear weight of 0.286 in log width for $(n,N)=(200,20000)$ and 0.178 for $(500,2000)$ (Table~\\ref{tab:rsens}), i.e.\\ roughly 25\\% to 16\\% shorter intervals; the gain falls as $r=n/N$ grows because the unlabeled sample then carries less of the variance. The family-level ceiling of 0.0416 is much smaller because three of the four regimes have no headroom and the evaluator's ratio of summed widths weights the nonlinear regime by its (smallest) interval width. For census income the same calculation gives at most 0.0034 over an unclipped linear weight, whatever $r$. % @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#oracle_recal_over_linear_by_cell]]\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Oracle recalibration gain over an unclipped linear weight on $\\hat Y$ (log width ratio, Eq.~\\eqref{eq:ceiling}) by sample-size cell, for the one synthetic regime with nonlinear calibration and for the census-income pool (derived).}\n\\label{tab:rsens}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\n & $(200,20000)$ & $(500,20000)$ & $(200,2000)$ & $(500,2000)$ \\\\\n$r=n/N$ & 0.010 & 0.025 & 0.100 & 0.250 \\\\\n\\midrule\nsynthetic, nonlinear regime & 0.2855 & 0.2750 & 0.2323 & 0.1776 \\\\\ncensus income & 0.0034 & 0.0034 & 0.0030 & 0.0025 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#oracle_recal_over_linear_by_cell]]\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Stability across evaluator partitions}\nThe evaluator also reports the aggregate score separately for five partitions of the episodes, defined by the candidate reset seed: 0.0247, 0.0220, 0.0203, 0.0213 and 0.0235, all positive. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\nThese values are a spread, not a confidence interval: the data draws do not depend on the reset seed, and because of the evaluator's episode ordering each partition contains 76 episodes of one family and six of each other family, so partitions differ in composition (Supplement~\\ref{sec:si-partition}). % @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#partition_composition_main_episodes]]\n\n\\subsection{Design-space contrast on the search pools}\\label{sec:search}\nFigure~\\ref{fig:search} compares the three model variants, each evaluated once on the search pools. Their aggregate scores against PPI++ are 0.0271 (recalibrated isotonic/binned), 0.0223 (stacked calibration) and 0.0046 (label-free basis with PRESS); all three were scored and passed the evaluator's validity gate. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#grounding_sources.result.top_program_01]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#grounding_sources.result.top_program_02]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#grounding_sources.result.top_program_03]]\nThe recalibrated variant is largest on the synthetic family (0.0757) and gene expression (0.0290), and smallest on galaxy morphology (0.0035). % @evidence[[source_evidence/search_metrics/recalibrated_metrics.json]]\nThe stacked variant tracks it on the synthetic family (0.0707) and gene expression (0.0278) but is smaller on deforestation and protein disorder; the smooth label-free basis is close to PPI++ on the synthetic family (0.0034), where the nonlinearity is the exponential regime of the search generator. % @evidence[[source_evidence/search_metrics/stacked_metrics.json]] @evidence[[source_evidence/search_metrics/label_free_basis_metrics.json]]\nSearch-pool pooled coverage was 0.943 for the recalibrated and the stacked variants and 0.950 for the basis variant, with minimum family coverage 0.933, 0.917 and 0.933 respectively. % @evidence[[source_evidence/search_metrics/recalibrated_metrics.json]] @evidence[[source_evidence/search_metrics/stacked_metrics.json]] @evidence[[source_evidence/search_metrics/label_free_basis_metrics.json]]\nThese are single evaluations without uncertainty estimates, and the variants differ in several components at once (recalibrator class, variance estimator, selection rule), so the contrast cannot attribute the difference to any one component. The stacked variant scored lower than the simpler recalibrated variant on the aggregate (0.0223 against 0.0271). We computed no headroom ceilings for the search pools, so we offer no explanation of this difference.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/search_variant_contrast.pdf}\n\\caption{Design-space contrast on the search pools: log width ratio against PPI++ for three model variants, five families, one evaluation per variant. Separate from, and not comparable in scale to, the sealed final results.}\n\\label{fig:search}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\\label{sec:discussion}\n\\paragraph{What the evidence supports.} With fixed inputs, an interval narrower than PPI++ exists in the families we tested, but the average margin is about 2\\% in width and the margin depends strongly on the pool: close to zero for the two other census pools (insurance, education) and a few percent for census income and the synthetic mixture. These margins come from the whole evaluated configuration (cross-fitted recalibration, Student-$t$ quantile, variance estimator and PPI++ fallback), none of whose components was ablated, so we do not attribute them to any single component. The population-level diagnostic is of the same sign and roughly the same size as the observed margin for the synthetic mixture and, less closely, for census income, but it predicts none for the plankton pool (a two-valued predictor whose unclipped optimal weight lies inside $[0,1]$), where the observed margin is 3.5\\%, and it does not account for the observed gain in four of five families.\n\n\\paragraph{What it does not support.} The evidence does not show that the narrowing is free: coverage point estimates are 0.934 pooled and 0.91 for the lowest family, below 0.95, and the baselines' coverage is unknown. The observed gain exceeds the ceiling in four of five families, so part of it is not accounted for by the population-level diagnostic, and for plankton in particular the gain cannot be attributed to nonlinear calibration. The paper does not claim superiority beyond the pools evaluated, validity under the selection rule beyond the empirical gate, or generalization to other estimands or to covariate shift.\n\n\\paragraph{Limitations.} (1) Estimand: only the population mean at $\\alpha=0.05$. (2) Sample sizes: $n=500$ on real pools and $n\\in\\{200,500\\}$ on synthetic draws; no sweep over $n$ or $N$, although the theory predicts that small $n$ erodes the gain. (3) Single sealed run, no confidence intervals on width ratios, no per-episode widths or per-family coverage beyond the minimum in the artifacts, and no controlled ablation of the recalibrator, the second control variate, the Student-$t$ quantile or the selection rule. The mechanisms discussed above are therefore hypotheses. (4) The ceiling is a population-level asymptotic quantity computed with the evaluator's finite pools as the population; it is a diagnostic, not a bound on a finite draw. (5) The truth for real pools is the pool mean, with draws from the pool with replacement, so the evaluation checks inference for that finite population rather than for the original scientific population. (6) The evaluator's diagnostics for the candidate are marked as unverified. (7) The selection rule makes the interval data-dependent and the 1\\% margin has no theoretical guarantee. (8) Stratified PPI is implemented but has no reported metrics and is not compared numerically.\n\n\\paragraph{Experiments that would test the hypotheses.} The mechanisms above can be tested with controlled comparisons that we did not run: (i) PPI++ with the weight clip removed, to separate the clipping component from nonlinear calibration; (ii) the recalibrated interval with the fallback disabled and with a fixed choice of recalibrator, to separate selection effects from the second control variate; (iii) the Student-$t$ quantile replaced by the normal quantile; (iv) coverage and width of every baseline, computed on the same draws and reported per family; and (v) a grid over $n$ and $N$ on the real pools, where the theory predicts a gain that shrinks with $n$ (estimation cost of order $p/n$) and with $r$. Until these are available, the decomposition in Section~\\ref{sec:headroomcheck} should be read as a diagnostic of what is possible, not as an attribution of what the evaluated interval does.\n\n\\paragraph{Practical reading.} As a population-level computation, for census income about 84\\% of the ceiling against PPI++ (0.0179 of 0.0213) is reachable by an unclipped linear weight on $\\Yhat$, whereas in the synthetic nonlinear regime the ceiling arises from the nonlinearity of $\\E[Y\\mid\\Yhat]$. This describes what is possible in the population, not what the evaluated interval does. Whether an unclipped PPI++ interval attains it, and at what coverage cost, was not tested here.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nWith labels, unlabeled data and predictor held fixed, the evaluated configuration (cross-fitted recalibration as a second control variate, Student-$t$ quantile and a PPI++ fallback) gave mean intervals 2.2\\% narrower than PPI++ on average in a sealed evaluation, with reductions between 0.2\\% and 4.0\\% by family, and 6.8\\% and 30\\% narrower than PPI with $\\lambda{=}1$ and the labeled-only interval. No component was ablated, so the gain is not attributed to recalibration. A closed-form population-level headroom diagnostic is small (aggregate 0.0129) and regime-dependent, and matches the observed margin closely only for the synthetic mixture and roughly for census income; it is zero for the plankton pool and does not account for the observed gain in four of five families. Coverage point estimates are 0.934 pooled and 0.91 for the lowest family, below the nominal 0.95, although the evaluator's tests did not reject. We read the result as conditional support for a small gain, not as evidence that PPI++ intervals can be shortened for free. The most useful next experiments are a coverage and width comparison of an unclipped PPI++ weight and each recalibration component in isolation, per-episode width and coverage reporting for all baselines, and a sweep over $n$.\n\n% IMPORTANT: \\clearpage flushes all pending floats before references so that\n% body-page counting excludes reference-only pages.  Do NOT remove.\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Information}\n\n\\subsection{Source evidence and implementation details}\\label{sec:si-source}\nImplementation statements in this paper refer to the readable files in \\texttt{source\\_evidence/}: \\texttt{frozen\\_winner\\_estimator.py} (the recalibrated interval), \\texttt{variant\\_label\\_free\\_basis\\_estimator.py} and \\texttt{variant\\_stacked\\_calibration\\_estimator.py} (the two contrast variants), \\texttt{evaluate\\_final.py} and \\texttt{evaluate\\_search.py} (the two evaluators; the first has the SHA-256 recorded by the sealed evaluation), and \\texttt{ppi\\_py\\_0.2.3\\_excerpt.txt} (the reference PPI++ weight, which is clipped to $[0,1]$ when \\texttt{clip=True}, the value used by the default mean interval). We assume the frozen file is the one evaluated in the sealed run, as recorded by the run manifest; hashes alone do not establish it.\n\nSettings of the evaluated interval: $K=5$ shuffled folds seeded from the candidate's random generator; isotonic fit with clipped out-of-bounds prediction; binned fit with $\\min(10,\\max(2,\\lfloor 0.8n/25\\rfloor))$ bins (10 for $n=500$, 6 for $n=200$); pooled standardization of features; ridge $10^{-6}\\,\\mathrm{tr}(A)/p$ on the weight system; Student-$t$ quantile via a Cornish--Fisher expansion with degrees of freedom floored at 3; PPI++ weight clipped to $[0,1]$; fallback margin 0.99 applied to the PPI++ half-width only (the narrower of the isotonic and binned candidates is kept, with no margin between them). The evaluator enforces a 3\\,s per-estimate deadline on the evaluated estimator.\n\n\\paragraph{Contrast variants.} The label-free basis variant uses a continuous piecewise-linear basis of $\\Yhat$ with knots at pooled quantiles, the model size $p\\in\\{1,2,3,5,8\\}$ is chosen by leave-one-out PRESS with a 2\\% improvement margin, the standard error uses leverage-corrected leave-one-out residuals, and a guard keeps the width ratio against PPI++ above 0.85. The stacked variant fits seven calibrators (isotonic, three binned with 3, 5 and 8 bins, monotone and local-linear kernels, linear), combines them by non-negative least squares with 4 inner folds, repeats $K=5$ cross-fitting $R=5$ times under a time budget, and uses averaged-residual variance plus a split-dispersion term with $n-3$ degrees of freedom.\n\n\\subsection{Variance derivation}\\label{sec:si-deriv}\nFor fixed $f,\\lambda$, the two terms of $\\hat\\theta_\\lambda$ are independent, so $V(\\lambda)=\\Var(Y-\\lambda^\\top f)/n+\\Var(\\lambda^\\top f)/N$. Expanding, $nV(\\lambda)=\\Var(Y)-2\\lambda^\\top c+\\lambda^\\top\\Sigma\\lambda+r\\lambda^\\top\\Sigma\\lambda$, a quadratic with minimizer $\\lambda^\\star=\\Sigma^{-1}c/(1+r)$ and minimum $\\Var(Y)-c^\\top\\Sigma^{-1}c/(1+r)$. For $f=\\Yhat$, $c^\\top\\Sigma^{-1}c=\\Cov(Y,\\Yhat)^2/\\Var(\\Yhat)=\\rho^2\\Var(Y)$. For $f=(\\Yhat,m(\\Yhat))$ the linear span contains $m$, and the squared multiple correlation of $Y$ on the span equals $\\eta^2$. The PPI++ weight with its clip uses $\\lambda=\\mathrm{clip}(\\Cov(Y,\\Yhat)/((1+r)\\Var(\\Yhat)),0,1)$, whose variance is the quadratic evaluated at that point. These formulas are what the headroom script evaluates (\\texttt{analysis/headroom.py}).\n\n\\subsection{Evaluator partitions}\\label{sec:si-partition}\nFigure~\\ref{fig:partition} shows the five partition scores. Replicating the evaluator's episode plan shows that, after the first 30 draws, every family keeps the same reset seed, so each partition holds 76 draws of one family and 6 of every other. The scores mix large and very small per-family samples and should be read as a rough spread only.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=0.62\\textwidth]{figures/posthoc/headroom_partition_scores/partition_scores.pdf}\n\\caption{Aggregate log width ratio against PPI++ for each evaluator partition (candidate reset seed), with the pooled aggregate (dashed). Not independent replications.}\n\\label{fig:partition}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{Search-pool metrics and sealed-evaluation details}\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Search-pool metrics for the three model variants (single evaluation each): log width ratio against PPI++ by family, aggregate score, and coverage.}\n\\label{tab:search}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrrrrr}\n\\toprule\nVariant & galax. & forest & gene & alpha. & synth. & agg. & cov.\\ pooled & cov.\\ min \\\\\n\\midrule\nrecalibrated & 0.0035 & 0.0168 & 0.0290 & 0.0104 & 0.0757 & 0.0271 & 0.943 & 0.933 \\\\\nstacked & 0.0036 & 0.0054 & 0.0278 & 0.0038 & 0.0707 & 0.0223 & 0.943 & 0.917 \\\\\nlabel-free basis & 0.0014 & 0.0024 & 0.0111 & 0.0049 & 0.0034 & 0.0046 & 0.950 & 0.933 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/search_metrics/recalibrated_metrics.json]] @evidence[[source_evidence/search_metrics/stacked_metrics.json]] @evidence[[source_evidence/search_metrics/label_free_basis_metrics.json]]\n\\end{table}\n\nSearch pools: galaxy morphology (Galaxy Zoo-type crowd labels, \\citealt{Willett2013GalaxyZ2}; $n=300$, $N=5000$), Amazon deforestation ($n=100$, $N=1000$), gene expression ($n=500$, $N=10000$), protein disorder ($n=300$, $N=5000$), and synthetic regimes, 60 draws per family, reset seeds 1001--1005. We cite an original data source only for the galaxy and census pools; the original sources of the plankton, deforestation, gene-expression and protein-disorder pools were not traced for this paper. The sealed evaluation plan declares 100 draws for each of five families (500 main episodes) plus 30 draws for each of two base pools and three control types (180 control episodes), 680 in total (constants \\texttt{REPS\\_MAIN} and \\texttt{REPS\\_CONTROL} in \\texttt{source\\_evidence/evaluate\\_final.py}). The five recorded partitions have durations that sum to about 1332 seconds, against a wall-clock limit of 5400 seconds in the same script. % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n\n\\paragraph{Population quantities for the headroom check.} Table~\\ref{tab:moments} lists the squared correlation and the correlation ratio per family.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Pool-level moments used for the ceiling (isotonic estimate of $\\eta^2$; synthetic rows are large-sample regime moments). $\\lambda^\\star$ is the unclipped optimal weight on $\\Yhat$ at $n=500$, $N=20000$, not applicable (---) to the synthetic regimes, which mix several $(n,N)$ cells.}\n\\label{tab:moments}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\nPool or regime & $\\rho^2$ & $\\eta^2$ & $\\eta^2-\\rho^2$ & $\\lambda^\\star$ \\\\\n\\midrule\ncensus income & 0.3691 & 0.3734 & 0.0044 & 1.342 \\\\\ncensus insurance & 0.4996 & 0.5005 & 0.0009 & 0.961 \\\\\ncensus education & 0.3921 & 0.3932 & 0.0011 & 0.953 \\\\\nplankton (binary $\\Yhat$) & 0.7004 & 0.7004 & 0.0000 & 0.841 \\\\\nsynthetic: linear calibrated & 0.5894 & 0.5895 & 0.0002 & --- \\\\\nsynthetic: nonlinear miscalibrated & 0.4831 & 0.7124 & 0.2292 & --- \\\\\nsynthetic: uninformative & 0.0000 & 0.0000 & 0.0000 & --- \\\\\nsynthetic: heteroscedastic & 0.7614 & 0.7616 & 0.0002 & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#real]] @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#synthetic_regimes]]\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Reproducibility}\nThe derived quantities are produced by \\texttt{analysis/headroom.py} (reads the pool files, the evaluator's protocol constants and the sealed metrics, writes \\texttt{analysis/headroom\\_derived.json}) and the figures by \\texttt{analysis/make\\_figures.py}; both run in seconds on a CPU with NumPy, SciPy, scikit-learn and Matplotlib. Figure~\\ref{fig:overview} is a schematic with no data. Table~\\ref{tab:final} and Figure~\\ref{fig:final} come from the sealed metrics plus the headroom file, Table~\\ref{tab:search} and Figure~\\ref{fig:search} from the search-pool metrics files, and Figure~\\ref{fig:partition} from the sealed per-partition scores. The sealed evaluation itself is the external evaluator \\texttt{evaluate\\_final.py} run once on the frozen estimator, so it cannot be regenerated with different draws here. The synthetic ceilings use a fixed-seed Monte Carlo of $2\\times10^6$ draws per regime.\n\n\\subsection{LLM Usage}\nA large language model agent system was used throughout this study. It proposed the estimator designs, wrote and debugged the estimator code, ran the search-pool evaluations, wrote the headroom analysis and figure scripts, and drafted this manuscript. The task definition, the data pools and both evaluators were provided externally and were not modified; the sealed evaluation was run by the evaluator on the frozen estimator. All numbers in this paper come from the evaluator outputs or from the scripts cited above.\n\n\\end{document}\n","numLines":161,"startLine":150,"totalLines":310}}}
