{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgw9jvgUGtpVkFwXoN6b3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017quyEjVMvqjZeQaEbp9Vpm","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/workspace/paper.tex","offset":96,"limit":56},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12512,"cache_read_input_tokens":45518,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":12512},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"64cbee98-e155-4c0c-b11f-e1ce1b3430d6","uuid":"f5ac0b91-bb2a-4012-a17a-80dc0a080899","timestamp":"2026-11-11T12:36:39.837Z","request_id":"req_011Cgw9jv9jR3VouCmijJBbm"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017quyEjVMvqjZeQaEbp9Vpm","type":"tool_result","content":"96\t\\begin{table}[t]\n97\t\\centering\n98\t\\caption{Interval constructions compared. ``Weight'' is the control-variate weight on the listed features.}\n99\t\\label{tab:estimators}\n100\t\\small\n101\t\\begin{tabular}{p{2.6cm}p{2.9cm}p{3.1cm}p{3.6cm}}\n102\t\\toprule\n103\tInterval & Features & Weight & Critical value / notes \\\\\n104\t\\midrule\n105\tLabeled-only & none & --- & normal; uses labels only \\\\\n106\tPPI & $\\Yhat$ & $\\lambda{=}1$ & normal \\\\\n107\tPPI++ & $\\Yhat$ & scalar, estimated, clipped to $[0,1]$ & normal; primary comparator, also the fallback \\\\\n108\tStratified PPI & quantile strata of $\\Yhat$ & $\\lambda{=}1$ per stratum & normal; implemented, no reported results \\\\\n109\tRecalibrated PPI (studied) & $\\Yhat$ and cross-fitted $g(\\Yhat)$ & vector, estimated, unclipped & Student-$t$, $n{-}1{-}p$ d.f.; adopted only if $\\ge1\\%$ shorter than PPI++ \\\\\n110\t\\bottomrule\n111\t\\end{tabular}\n112\t\\end{table}\n113\t\n114\t\\subsection{Cross-fitted recalibrated PPI}\n115\tLet $K=5$. Labeled indices are split at random into $K$ folds. For each fold, a recalibrator $g^{(k)}$ is fitted on the other folds. Out-of-fold values $g^{(k)}(\\Yhat_i)$ are used on the held-out labeled points, and the average $K^{-1}\\sum_k g^{(k)}(\\Yhat_j)$ is used on the unlabeled points. Two recalibrators are used: an isotonic fit whose direction follows the sign of the sample correlation, and a quantile-binned conditional mean with $\\min(10,\\max(2,\\lfloor 0.8n/25\\rfloor))$ bins. The two features $(\\Yhat,g)$ are standardized with their pooled mean and standard deviation, and the weight solves $\\lambda=(\\hat\\Sigma_\\ell+r\\hat\\Sigma_u+\\epsilon I)^{-1}\\hat c$ with a small ridge $\\epsilon$. The interval is the control-variate estimate plus or minus a Student-$t$ quantile with $n-1-p$ degrees of freedom ($p=2$) times $\\sqrt{s_u^2/N+s_\\ell^2/n}$, where $s_\\ell^2$ is the residual variance on labeled points and $s_u^2$ the variance of the weighted control variate on unlabeled points.\n116\t\n117\t\\paragraph{Selection with fallback.} The PPI++ interval is computed first. A recalibrated candidate is adopted only if its half-width is below 0.99 times the PPI++ half-width. The isotonic candidate is tried first and the binned candidate second; once the isotonic candidate has been adopted, the binned candidate replaces it if it is merely narrower, with no additional margin. If neither candidate passes the test against PPI++, PPI++ is returned. If $n<20$ or $\\Yhat$ is constant, no candidate is formed. The 1\\% margin is a heuristic guard against selection noise, not a validity guarantee; the interval chosen by comparing estimated widths is data-dependent, and the standard-error estimate does not account for the estimation of $g$ or of $\\lambda$ beyond the degrees-of-freedom correction.\n118\t\n119\t\\subsection{Evaluation protocol}\n120\t\\paragraph{Pools and draws.} The sealed evaluation uses four real pools, each with $n=500$ labeled and $N=20000$ unlabeled records: California census income, census private-insurance indicator, a census education-related quantity (all with model predictions; census tabular benchmarks of the kind assembled by \\citealt{Ding2021RetiringAN}), and a plankton-imaging pool whose prediction takes only two values. A fifth family is synthetic with four regimes (linear calibrated, nonlinear miscalibrated with a cubic predictor, uninformative, heteroscedastic), $n\\in\\{200,500\\}$ and $N\\in\\{2000,20000\\}$. Each family has 100 independent draws; real-pool draws are taken with replacement from the pool, so the truth is the pool mean, and synthetic truths are analytic. Controls with a constant predictor, a permuted predictor and a shifted outcome are run separately.\n121\t\n122\t\\paragraph{Score and validity gate.} For each family $f$ the evaluator forms $s_f=-\\log(\\sum_e W_e/\\sum_e P_e)$ from candidate widths $W_e$ and PPI++ widths $P_e$ over its episodes (positive means narrower), and averages $s_f$ over the five families. The same construction against PPI ($\\lambda{=}1$) and the labeled-only interval gives the other two comparators; baselines come from ppi-python 0.2.3. Validity requires non-rejection of nominal coverage by exact one-sided binomial tests (pooled level 0.01, per-family level $0.01/5$, failed episodes counted as misses with worst-case width) and all controls passing. A paired episode-level bootstrap of the aggregate score (2000 replicates) must have a positive 2.5th percentile for the narrowing to be declared. Non-rejection is a weaker statement than coverage equal to nominal.\n123\t\n124\t\\paragraph{Design-space contrast.} Before the sealed evaluation, three model variants were scored once each on separate search pools (galaxy morphology, Amazon deforestation, gene expression, protein disorder, plus synthetic regimes; 60 draws per family): the recalibrated interval above, a label-free spline-basis control variate with leave-one-out PRESS model selection, and a repeated-cross-fitted calibrator stack combined by non-negative least squares. These are single evaluations on search pools and are reported as a contrast in Section~\\ref{sec:search}, not as controlled ablations; the search pools are disjoint from the sealed pools.\n125\t\n126\t\\section{Results}\\label{sec:results}\n127\t\\subsection{Validity: coverage evidence}\\label{sec:validity}\n128\tThe sealed evaluation completed with all controls passed, zero failed episodes, and the validity gate and the bootstrap criterion satisfied (final outcome \\emph{qualified}). Pooled empirical coverage over the 500 evaluated draws is 0.934 (467 covered) and the lowest family coverage is 0.91 (91 of 100). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.coverage_pooled]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.coverage_min_family]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.controls_passed]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.n_failed_episodes]]\n129\tBoth point estimates lie below the nominal 0.95. Under exact binomial calculations with $p=0.95$, the lower-tail probabilities of the observed counts are 0.066 (pooled) and 0.063 (lowest family), % @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#coverage]]\n130\twhich is why the gate did not reject, but also not strong support for exact nominal coverage; the Monte Carlo standard errors are 0.011 and 0.029. % @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#coverage]]\n131\tCoverage of the PPI++ and labeled-only baselines was not recorded in the available artifacts, and per-family coverage other than the minimum is unavailable. We therefore cannot say whether the shortfall is a property of recalibration, of finite-sample Wald intervals at these sample sizes, or of the data-dependent selection step.\n132\t\n133\t\\subsection{Width: sealed final evaluation}\n134\tFigure~\\ref{fig:final} and Table~\\ref{tab:final} give the width results. Against PPI++ the mean log width ratio is 0.022, i.e.\\ the geometric-mean interval is 2.2\\% narrower (derived as $1-e^{-0.022}$). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.combined_score]]\n135\tAgainst PPI with $\\lambda{=}1$ the mean log ratio is 0.070 (6.8\\% narrower) and against the labeled-only interval 0.358 (30\\% narrower). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.log_ratio_vs_ppi_lambda1_mean]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.log_ratio_vs_classical_mean]]\n136\tMost of the gain over labeled-only inference is therefore already captured by PPI machinery; recalibration adds a small increment on top. Per family, the narrowing against PPI++ is 0.0287 for census income, 0.0018 for census insurance, 0.0023 for census education, 0.0359 for plankton and 0.0413 for the synthetic mixture, corresponding to derived width reductions of 2.8\\%, 0.2\\%, 0.2\\%, 3.5\\% and 4.0\\%. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics]] @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#derived_width_reduction_vs_ppi_pp]]\n137\tNo family is wider than PPI++ on this measure, but the gain is near zero for two of five families.\n138\t\n139\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n140\t\\centering\n141\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/final_width_ratio.pdf}\n142\t\\caption{Sealed final width results. (a) Observed log width ratio against PPI++ by family (bars; positive means narrower than PPI++) and the population-level oracle ceiling of Eq.~\\eqref{eq:ceiling} (diamonds, derived from the pools). (b) Aggregate mean log ratio against the three comparators, with the aggregate ceiling. Single sealed evaluation; no confidence intervals are available for the ratios.}\n143\t\\label{fig:final}\n144\t\\end{figure}\n145\t\n146\t\\begin{table}[t]\n147\t\\centering\n148\t\\caption{Sealed final results by family: observed log width ratio against PPI++, derived percent reduction, oracle ceiling of Eq.~\\eqref{eq:ceiling} on the pool (derived), the part of the ceiling reachable by an unclipped linear weight, and the excess of observed over ceiling. Aggregate row: mean over families.}\n149\t\\label{tab:final}\n150\t\\small\n151\t\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrr}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"64cbee98-e155-4c0c-b11f-e1ce1b3430d6","uuid":"068ba817-a6ab-47cc-9233-029f14f29603","timestamp":"2026-11-11T12:36:39.857Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/paper.tex","content":"\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Interval constructions compared. ``Weight'' is the control-variate weight on the listed features.}\n\\label{tab:estimators}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{p{2.6cm}p{2.9cm}p{3.1cm}p{3.6cm}}\n\\toprule\nInterval & Features & Weight & Critical value / notes \\\\\n\\midrule\nLabeled-only & none & --- & normal; uses labels only \\\\\nPPI & $\\Yhat$ & $\\lambda{=}1$ & normal \\\\\nPPI++ & $\\Yhat$ & scalar, estimated, clipped to $[0,1]$ & normal; primary comparator, also the fallback \\\\\nStratified PPI & quantile strata of $\\Yhat$ & $\\lambda{=}1$ per stratum & normal; implemented, no reported results \\\\\nRecalibrated PPI (studied) & $\\Yhat$ and cross-fitted $g(\\Yhat)$ & vector, estimated, unclipped & Student-$t$, $n{-}1{-}p$ d.f.; adopted only if $\\ge1\\%$ shorter than PPI++ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Cross-fitted recalibrated PPI}\nLet $K=5$. Labeled indices are split at random into $K$ folds. For each fold, a recalibrator $g^{(k)}$ is fitted on the other folds. Out-of-fold values $g^{(k)}(\\Yhat_i)$ are used on the held-out labeled points, and the average $K^{-1}\\sum_k g^{(k)}(\\Yhat_j)$ is used on the unlabeled points. Two recalibrators are used: an isotonic fit whose direction follows the sign of the sample correlation, and a quantile-binned conditional mean with $\\min(10,\\max(2,\\lfloor 0.8n/25\\rfloor))$ bins. The two features $(\\Yhat,g)$ are standardized with their pooled mean and standard deviation, and the weight solves $\\lambda=(\\hat\\Sigma_\\ell+r\\hat\\Sigma_u+\\epsilon I)^{-1}\\hat c$ with a small ridge $\\epsilon$. The interval is the control-variate estimate plus or minus a Student-$t$ quantile with $n-1-p$ degrees of freedom ($p=2$) times $\\sqrt{s_u^2/N+s_\\ell^2/n}$, where $s_\\ell^2$ is the residual variance on labeled points and $s_u^2$ the variance of the weighted control variate on unlabeled points.\n\n\\paragraph{Selection with fallback.} The PPI++ interval is computed first. A recalibrated candidate is adopted only if its half-width is below 0.99 times the PPI++ half-width. The isotonic candidate is tried first and the binned candidate second; once the isotonic candidate has been adopted, the binned candidate replaces it if it is merely narrower, with no additional margin. If neither candidate passes the test against PPI++, PPI++ is returned. If $n<20$ or $\\Yhat$ is constant, no candidate is formed. The 1\\% margin is a heuristic guard against selection noise, not a validity guarantee; the interval chosen by comparing estimated widths is data-dependent, and the standard-error estimate does not account for the estimation of $g$ or of $\\lambda$ beyond the degrees-of-freedom correction.\n\n\\subsection{Evaluation protocol}\n\\paragraph{Pools and draws.} The sealed evaluation uses four real pools, each with $n=500$ labeled and $N=20000$ unlabeled records: California census income, census private-insurance indicator, a census education-related quantity (all with model predictions; census tabular benchmarks of the kind assembled by \\citealt{Ding2021RetiringAN}), and a plankton-imaging pool whose prediction takes only two values. A fifth family is synthetic with four regimes (linear calibrated, nonlinear miscalibrated with a cubic predictor, uninformative, heteroscedastic), $n\\in\\{200,500\\}$ and $N\\in\\{2000,20000\\}$. Each family has 100 independent draws; real-pool draws are taken with replacement from the pool, so the truth is the pool mean, and synthetic truths are analytic. Controls with a constant predictor, a permuted predictor and a shifted outcome are run separately.\n\n\\paragraph{Score and validity gate.} For each family $f$ the evaluator forms $s_f=-\\log(\\sum_e W_e/\\sum_e P_e)$ from candidate widths $W_e$ and PPI++ widths $P_e$ over its episodes (positive means narrower), and averages $s_f$ over the five families. The same construction against PPI ($\\lambda{=}1$) and the labeled-only interval gives the other two comparators; baselines come from ppi-python 0.2.3. Validity requires non-rejection of nominal coverage by exact one-sided binomial tests (pooled level 0.01, per-family level $0.01/5$, failed episodes counted as misses with worst-case width) and all controls passing. A paired episode-level bootstrap of the aggregate score (2000 replicates) must have a positive 2.5th percentile for the narrowing to be declared. Non-rejection is a weaker statement than coverage equal to nominal.\n\n\\paragraph{Design-space contrast.} Before the sealed evaluation, three model variants were scored once each on separate search pools (galaxy morphology, Amazon deforestation, gene expression, protein disorder, plus synthetic regimes; 60 draws per family): the recalibrated interval above, a label-free spline-basis control variate with leave-one-out PRESS model selection, and a repeated-cross-fitted calibrator stack combined by non-negative least squares. These are single evaluations on search pools and are reported as a contrast in Section~\\ref{sec:search}, not as controlled ablations; the search pools are disjoint from the sealed pools.\n\n\\section{Results}\\label{sec:results}\n\\subsection{Validity: coverage evidence}\\label{sec:validity}\nThe sealed evaluation completed with all controls passed, zero failed episodes, and the validity gate and the bootstrap criterion satisfied (final outcome \\emph{qualified}). Pooled empirical coverage over the 500 evaluated draws is 0.934 (467 covered) and the lowest family coverage is 0.91 (91 of 100). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.coverage_pooled]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.coverage_min_family]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.controls_passed]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.n_failed_episodes]]\nBoth point estimates lie below the nominal 0.95. Under exact binomial calculations with $p=0.95$, the lower-tail probabilities of the observed counts are 0.066 (pooled) and 0.063 (lowest family), % @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#coverage]]\nwhich is why the gate did not reject, but also not strong support for exact nominal coverage; the Monte Carlo standard errors are 0.011 and 0.029. % @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#coverage]]\nCoverage of the PPI++ and labeled-only baselines was not recorded in the available artifacts, and per-family coverage other than the minimum is unavailable. We therefore cannot say whether the shortfall is a property of recalibration, of finite-sample Wald intervals at these sample sizes, or of the data-dependent selection step.\n\n\\subsection{Width: sealed final evaluation}\nFigure~\\ref{fig:final} and Table~\\ref{tab:final} give the width results. Against PPI++ the mean log width ratio is 0.022, i.e.\\ the geometric-mean interval is 2.2\\% narrower (derived as $1-e^{-0.022}$). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.combined_score]]\nAgainst PPI with $\\lambda{=}1$ the mean log ratio is 0.070 (6.8\\% narrower) and against the labeled-only interval 0.358 (30\\% narrower). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.log_ratio_vs_ppi_lambda1_mean]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.log_ratio_vs_classical_mean]]\nMost of the gain over labeled-only inference is therefore already captured by PPI machinery; recalibration adds a small increment on top. Per family, the narrowing against PPI++ is 0.0287 for census income, 0.0018 for census insurance, 0.0023 for census education, 0.0359 for plankton and 0.0413 for the synthetic mixture, corresponding to derived width reductions of 2.8\\%, 0.2\\%, 0.2\\%, 3.5\\% and 4.0\\%. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics]] @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#derived_width_reduction_vs_ppi_pp]]\nNo family is wider than PPI++ on this measure, but the gain is near zero for two of five families.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/final_width_ratio.pdf}\n\\caption{Sealed final width results. (a) Observed log width ratio against PPI++ by family (bars; positive means narrower than PPI++) and the population-level oracle ceiling of Eq.~\\eqref{eq:ceiling} (diamonds, derived from the pools). (b) Aggregate mean log ratio against the three comparators, with the aggregate ceiling. Single sealed evaluation; no confidence intervals are available for the ratios.}\n\\label{fig:final}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Sealed final results by family: observed log width ratio against PPI++, derived percent reduction, oracle ceiling of Eq.~\\eqref{eq:ceiling} on the pool (derived), the part of the ceiling reachable by an unclipped linear weight, and the excess of observed over ceiling. Aggregate row: mean over families.}\n\\label{tab:final}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrr}","numLines":56,"startLine":96,"totalLines":310}}}
